# rebucket-fuses.rq — box-split Task 3b (AXIOM: derivation, open world, evidence-positive).
# Spec: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-28-box-split-design.md § 8.6 (supersedes § 8.2's witness).
#
# A FUSION WITNESS over a ruled band: a cell c the rule re-bucket actually FORMS (a word of
# geometry.rule_aware_lines' output, at the extent of its own non-space glyphs) whose extent
# [c.x0, c.x1) contains a point covered by no word of any line of the band's own word layout
# (a word v covers p iff v.x0 <= p < v.x1). Such a cell joins ink from both sides of a gap the
# band's words leave open: re-bucketing on those rules would FUSE what the layout separates. The
# witness quantifies over the OUTPUT, not a model of it, so how the cell was formed (edge columns
# extended to the ink, dividers dropped per row by R154, bucketing by glyph centre) is covered by
# construction.
#
# SPARQL has no point quantifier, so the uncovered point is reached through a finite candidate
# set: an uncovered point exists in [c.x0, c.x1) iff some candidate e in {c.x0} U {w.x1 : w a
# layout word} has c.x0 <= e < c.x1 and is uncovered. Take the largest candidate e* <= p: a word
# covering e* ends past it, so either it ends at or before p (contradicting maximality) or it
# covers p. c.x0 is a candidate so that a glyph belonging to no word cannot hide a gap (it can only
# make the witness fire, which keeps the refusal). Below, EVERY formed cell's x0 is a candidate
# for every cell: that only adds points of the band, and any uncovered point inside c's extent is
# a witness by definition, so the answer is unchanged.
#
# Used by compile._build_ruled_band as a CONJUNCT of the R225 count: the refusal stands only where
# a witness is PRESENT (evidence-positive). Its one NOT EXISTS is closed within the band's own
# fresh evidence graph, which is the closure boundary (as in confirm-boundary.rq). Threshold-free:
# every bound is data; NO numeric literal (test_no_tuned_constant_in_rq_files).
#
# The two sub-selects are DISTINCT for EVALUATION COST, not meaning (ASK is true or false either
# way). Without it rdflib joins them lazily and re-evaluates the second, NOT EXISTS included,
# once per solution of the first (measured on § 8.2's form: 257 s without DISTINCT on a synthetic
# 17 x 8 band, about 1 s with it). With it each side is evaluated once.
PREFIX tab: <https://w3id.org/iladub/tab#>
ASK {
    {
        SELECT DISTINCT ?cx0 ?cx1 WHERE {
            ?c a tab:FormedCell ; tab:formedCellX0 ?cx0 ; tab:formedCellX1 ?cx1 .
        }
    }
    {
        SELECT DISTINCT ?edge WHERE {
            { ?k a tab:FormedCell ; tab:formedCellX0 ?edge . }
            UNION
            { ?w a tab:LayoutWord ; tab:layoutWordX1 ?edge . }
            FILTER NOT EXISTS {
                ?v a tab:LayoutWord ; tab:layoutWordX0 ?vx0 ; tab:layoutWordX1 ?vx1 .
                FILTER(?vx0 <= ?edge && ?edge < ?vx1)
            }
        }
    }
    FILTER(?cx0 <= ?edge && ?edge < ?cx1)
}
